hi my name is juana domitrescu i come  from romania  currently i'm working in the european  commission in the department working on  education culture youth and sports i  work in the higher education unit where  we are working on the implementation and  development of the erasmus plus program  i think the role of  exchange programs of mobility programs  such as erasmus plus or european  solidarity core  is  not to promote  one set of european identity values  for young people  i think the whole idea is that it  offers people the chance  to have a hands-on experience in a  different society in a different  community to be of service in that  particular community so  you see that a new thing in its own has  been kind of established by new  generations going into this program and  defining what  european values mean to them  i think it was exactly my experience as  an international student  and the fact that i was studying and  researching european identity and i was  volunteering in an organization where  people identify themselves as european a  lot  that made me want to continue this in my  professional  trajectory  so what i found is that there's two ways  of looking at european identity so of  course there's this traditional way of  understanding european identity which  stems a lot from kind of philosophical  thought and political thought of the  ages on the idea of building a europe  based on freedom so this notion actually  stems from immanuel kant as well from  the 18th century but of course it it was  even more poignant um  in light of the sec the the two world  wars and the way this really impacted  europe in devastated europe so it was  initially a political project of coming  together and establishing some european  values that we all believe in and some  goals that we can achieve together the  idea that based on freedom democracy  um  and peace we can work together to build  more than if we are at war with one  another  so as you can see this is all it really  sounds a bit top down right because this  is kind of a political approach that has  a lot of value but that doesn't  necessarily  come from the culture or or for kind of  from from society as as such from a  bottom-up approach  so that was the main challenge actually  that is the main challenge when you talk  about european identity because with  years of freedom  you have people kind of taking ownership  of  being european because they live in this  free society and they are willing and  able to try to travel and experience  different countries  so this is more constructivist approach  which looks more at  how people or european citizens  themselves identify themselves with  europe or with european values  and what do they consider to be european  or not european  what happens with short-term exchanges  such as erasmus plus or european  solidarity core  is that  it first puts people in contact with a  different culture with a different  language and it gets them to experience  a different place  in a very different way from when you're  just going on holiday because you really  have to immerse yourself in the new  society you have to come across  different ideas different ways of doing  things and that really  forces you to kind of react and change  yourself in the process which is where  the notion of european identity comes in  it ultimately equips  especially young people with skills and  views that they can employ in their  host their home society  which has this effect of  internationalization at home  meaning that you know  you're not only the one who are changed  but you can go on and change and bring  these new ideas in your home society as  well
 

